1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,240
Good morning! Today, I want to take you
through the early history of photography,

2
00:00:04,619 --> 00:00:07,214
from the very first permanent
image right up to the

3
00:00:07,238 --> 00:00:09,692
digital cameras we all
carry in our pockets now.

4
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,580
So, where does it all
begin? Most historians

5
00:00:12,604 --> 00:00:15,112
agree that the first
permanent photograph was

6
00:00:15,113 --> 00:00:17,951
produced in 1826 by a
French inventor named

7
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:20,960
Nicephore Niepce. It's
worth remembering that

8
00:00:20,961 --> 00:00:23,966
date carefully, because
some textbooks mistakenly

9
00:00:23,990 --> 00:00:27,132
place it a decade earlier,
around 1816, when Niepce

10
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:30,420
was really only experimenting
with early, unstable

11
00:00:30,444 --> 00:00:32,925
images that faded
almost immediately.

12
00:00:32,926 --> 00:00:36,228
The 1826 image, by contrast,
actually survived, and you

13
00:00:36,252 --> 00:00:38,385
can still see it in
a museum today.

14
00:00:38,386 --> 00:00:41,804
Now, what's remarkable, and
slightly hard to imagine, is just

15
00:00:41,828 --> 00:00:44,472
how long that first
photograph took to capture.

16
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:48,090
The exposure time required
for it was about eight hours.

17
00:00:48,114 --> 00:00:50,467
Not eight minutes,
and certainly not eight

18
00:00:50,468 --> 00:00:53,204
days, but a full working
day of sunlight falling

19
00:00:53,228 --> 00:00:55,812
onto the plate before
an image formed at all.

20
00:00:56,229 --> 00:00:58,832
Building on Niepce's
work, his collaborator Louis

21
00:00:58,856 --> 00:01:01,472
Daguerre developed a
much more practical process.

22
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,593
The daguerreotype process
was announced to the

23
00:01:04,617 --> 00:01:07,292
public in France in 1839,
and it caused an immediate

24
00:01:07,293 --> 00:01:10,568
sensation across Europe.
However, the daguerreotype

25
00:01:10,592 --> 00:01:13,892
did have one major drawback.
Each image was unique.

26
00:01:14,239 --> 00:01:16,758
There was no negative, so
you couldn't make copies.

27
00:01:16,782 --> 00:01:19,990
If you wanted two portraits, you
had to sit for the camera twice.

28
00:01:19,991 --> 00:01:23,892
This is quite different from a common
misconception that the problem was fading.

29
00:01:24,329 --> 00:01:26,959
In fact, well-preserved
daguerreotypes have lasted

30
00:01:26,983 --> 00:01:29,567
remarkably well. This
limitation was addressed by

31
00:01:29,568 --> 00:01:32,281
the callotype process,
developed by the Englishman

32
00:01:32,305 --> 00:01:35,165
William Henry Fox Talbot.
Unlike the daguerreotype,

33
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,891
the callotype allowed
multiple copies to be made

34
00:01:37,915 --> 00:01:40,507
from a single paper negative,
which was a genuinely

35
00:01:40,508 --> 00:01:43,882
revolutionary step forward for
photography as a mass medium.

36
00:01:43,906 --> 00:01:45,912
Moving into the
later 19th century,

37
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:49,145
glass plates were the standard
support for photographic

38
00:01:49,169 --> 00:01:51,572
chemicals for many
decades. But in the 1880s,

39
00:01:51,734 --> 00:01:54,286
flexible film began to
replace glass plates, making

40
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:56,672
cameras dramatically
lighter and more portable.

41
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,875
And that brings us to
George Eastman, a name most

42
00:01:59,899 --> 00:02:02,747
of you will recognize.
Eastman is best known not for

43
00:02:02,748 --> 00:02:05,173
inventing a new chemical
process, but for making

44
00:02:05,197 --> 00:02:07,692
cameras affordable and
simple enough for ordinary

45
00:02:07,829 --> 00:02:10,723
people to use. His Kodak
company turned photography

46
00:02:10,747 --> 00:02:13,312
from a specialist trade
into a popular hobby.

47
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:16,505
In fact, the slogan for
those early Kodak cameras

48
00:02:16,529 --> 00:02:19,072
captures this perfectly.
You press the button,

49
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:21,636
we do the rest. The
customer simply took the

50
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:24,047
photographs, sent the
whole camera back to the

51
00:02:24,048 --> 00:02:27,178
factory, and Kodak handled
the developing and printing.

52
00:02:27,202 --> 00:02:29,947
Now let's turn to color.
Early color photography

53
00:02:29,948 --> 00:02:32,545
mainly used a process
called autochrome, patented

54
00:02:32,569 --> 00:02:35,788
by the Lumiere brothers in
France in the early 20th century.

55
00:02:35,789 --> 00:02:38,858
The autochrome process
is fascinating technically.

56
00:02:38,882 --> 00:02:43,414
It used tiny grains of dyed potato starch
spread across a glass plate, which acted

57
00:02:43,438 --> 00:02:46,576
as microscopic color filters
when light passed through them.

58
00:02:46,614 --> 00:02:50,972
Despite its charm, autochrome never became
hugely popular with the general public,

59
00:02:51,210 --> 00:02:53,783
and what limited it was
really both cost and long

60
00:02:53,807 --> 00:02:56,547
exposure times together.
It wasn't simply one factor

61
00:02:56,548 --> 00:02:58,988
or the other, but the
combination of the two that

62
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:01,372
kept it a specialist,
rather expensive process.

63
00:03:01,857 --> 00:03:04,386
The real breakthrough for
ordinary consumers came

64
00:03:04,410 --> 00:03:07,047
in 1935 with Kodachrome
film, which is notable for

65
00:03:07,048 --> 00:03:09,954
being the first successful
color film for amateurs.

66
00:03:09,978 --> 00:03:12,732
Suddenly, families could
take color snapshots at

67
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:14,928
home, not just in a
professional studio.

68
00:03:14,952 --> 00:03:17,607
Photojournalism, meanwhile,
grew rapidly once smaller,

69
00:03:17,608 --> 00:03:20,050
portable cameras appeared.
Reporters could finally

70
00:03:20,074 --> 00:03:22,727
follow events as they
unfolded, rather than setting up

71
00:03:22,728 --> 00:03:25,018
bulky equipment on
a tripod beforehand.

72
00:03:25,042 --> 00:03:28,731
Much of that portability came
down to a particular film format.

73
00:03:28,732 --> 00:03:32,093
The 35mm format became
popular mainly because it was

74
00:03:32,117 --> 00:03:35,360
compact and convenient,
allowing many exposures on

75
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:38,778
a single roll of film in a camera
small enough to carry anywhere.

76
00:03:38,802 --> 00:03:40,831
Now, jumping ahead
to the digital era.

77
00:03:40,832 --> 00:03:43,068
The first digital camera
was developed in

78
00:03:43,092 --> 00:03:45,612
a company research
lab, specifically at Kodak,

79
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:48,734
by an engineer named
Stephen Sasson back in 1975.

80
00:03:48,758 --> 00:03:52,407
Early digital cameras, however,
were mainly limited by image

81
00:03:52,408 --> 00:03:55,254
storage capacity. That first
prototype could hold only

82
00:03:55,278 --> 00:03:57,927
a handful of low resolution
black and white images

83
00:03:57,928 --> 00:04:00,643
on a cassette tape. Fast
forward to the smartphone

84
00:04:00,667 --> 00:04:03,287
era, and what's changed
is less about the camera

85
00:04:03,288 --> 00:04:05,928
technology itself, and
more about behavior.

86
00:04:05,952 --> 00:04:09,692
Smartphone cameras mainly
changed how photographs are shared,

87
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:13,520
letting people post images instantly
to friends and family around the world.

88
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:16,516
So, to sum up, this
lecture concludes that the

89
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:21,178
biggest shift in photography's history has
been from chemical to digital processes.

90
00:04:21,202 --> 00:04:24,727
A change even bigger, arguably,
than the earlier move from

91
00:04:24,728 --> 00:04:27,756
monochrome to color.
Finally, I want you to note two

92
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:31,385
key factors that drove this shift
towards digital photography.

93
00:04:31,386 --> 00:04:33,278
The first was
falling memory card

94
00:04:33,302 --> 00:04:36,407
prices, which made storing
thousands of digital images

95
00:04:36,408 --> 00:04:38,681
cheap and practical.
And the second was the

96
00:04:38,705 --> 00:04:41,372
growth of the internet,
which gave people an easy,

97
00:04:41,410 --> 00:04:44,171
immediate way to share
those digital photographs with

98
00:04:44,195 --> 00:04:46,807
the whole world. Those
two factors together really

99
00:04:46,808 --> 00:04:49,320
explain why digital
overtook films so quickly.

100
00:04:49,344 --> 00:04:53,052
Right, let's take a short break
before we look at some case studies.

